     },
     "GlobalBuffer": {
      "modelled": "2048 x 256b, 4 banks, datawidth 8",
      "declared_claim": "Carries Outputs at 8b, BELOW the 24b accumulator, and says so in the YAML with a `# psum-width-ok:` note: the PE post-processing unit requantizes on the way out of PEAccuBuffer, so this level holds activations rather than partial sums.",
      "not_verified": "The buffer and PE geometries below the MAC come from the exercises repo, not from a table checked against the paper. They are a REPRODUCTION, not a citation. Only the precisions above have been traced to the paper."
     },
     "PEWeightBuffer + PEAccuBuffer + PEInputBuffer + PEWeightRegs": {
      "modelled": "as shipped by timeloop-accelergy-exercises",
      "not_verified": "same caveat as GlobalBuffer",
      "contradicts_the_paper": "Upgraded 2026-09-06 from \"unverified\" to \"demonstrably not the published chiplet\", on two checks that need only numbers already verified here.\n(1) MAC COUNT. 16 PEs x 4 distributed_buffers x 4 reg_mac = 256 MACs. The paper's headline is \"4 tera-ops per second (TOPS) using 8-bit weights and activations\"; at 2 ops per MAC and Simba's published ~1.8 GHz that needs ~1024 MACs. This is a quarter-scale chiplet.\n(2) BUFFER HIERARCHY. As declared: PEInputBuffer 64 kB x 16 = 1 MB, PEWeightBuffer 32 kB x 64 = 2 MB, behind a GlobalBuffer of 64 kB. 3 MB of PE-private SRAM behind a shared buffer 48x smaller is not a memory hierarchy any real design has; a per-PE buffer is normally a small fraction of the shared one. A secondary source reproducing the paper's PE figure gives 32 KB / 8 KB / 3 KB for the three PE buffers, which would make PEInputBuffer 8-21x too large -- but that is a lecture slide, not the paper, so it is NOT used to re-dimension anything.\nCONSEQUENCE, and why this is not cosmetic: the geometry gives this design 2,113,536 on-chip weights, 28x eyeriss_like's 75,264. On-chip weight capacity is what sets DRAM refetch, and DRAM refetch is what sets the ECC saving. This design's 1.4x refetch and its saving are both products of a number that reproduces nothing.\nACTION TAKEN: `source: published` -> `source: reference_design` in standard.yaml, and the figure label \"Simba\" -> \"Simba-like (reference design)\". The geometry is UNCHANGED, because changing it without the paper would replace one unsourced number with another.",
      "to_fix_properly": "Obtain MICRO-52 Fig. 4 / Table 1 and re-dimension PEInputBuffer, PEAccuBuffer and the PEWeightBuffer instance count, then restore `source: published`. Attempted 2026-09-06 without success: ACM DL and CACM return 403, the Berkeley mirror 302s to a dead host, Semantic Scholar reports no open-access PDF."
     }
